---
import ProseLayout from '@/layouts/ProseLayout.astro';
---

<ProseLayout
  eyebrow="Corrections"
  title="How we correct the record"
  description="How Runtime Signals handles factual errors and material updates."
  canonicalPath="/corrections"
  traceSeed={17}
>
  <p>
    When a published claim turns out to be wrong, outdated, or missing an important limitation, we
    fix it in place. The article keeps its URL. The fix carries a visible revision note and an
    updated timestamp, and the original claim is never quietly edited away.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every revision, correction or not, is recorded on the article itself under "Revision history"
    with one of these badges, exactly as they appear there:
  </p>
  <ul class="badge-key">
    <li><span class="revision-badge" data-type="published">published</span> the original piece.</li>
    <li>
      <span class="revision-badge" data-type="update">update</span> a routine content or dependency fix.
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="revision-badge" data-type="correction">correction</span> a factual error, fixed and
      disclosed.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    See, for example, <a
      href="/articles/model-handoff-as-distributed-state-transfer#revision-history"
      >the revision history on our handoff piece</a
    >. No factual correction has been needed yet; when one is, it will carry a visible notice at the
    top of the article, not just an entry at the bottom.
  </p>
  <p>
    Retiring a piece - because a tool it covers no longer exists, or its practice no longer holds -
    is a separate action from a correction. Retired pieces are marked archived, stay reachable at
    their original URL with a notice, and drop out of the active feed and search index.
  </p>
  <p>
    No article moves in either direction without human review; a routine content or dependency fix
    does not require the same review a factual correction does, but neither is automated end to end.
  </p>
</ProseLayout>

<style>
  .badge-key {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin: var(--space-5) 0;
  }

  /* Exactly ArticleLayout.astro's .article__revision-badge, reused here so a
     reader sees the real badge before ever reaching an article - not a
     reinvented look-alike. */
  .revision-badge {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-1);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 0.15em 0.5em;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    border: 1px solid currentColor;
    margin-right: var(--space-2);
  }

  .revision-badge[data-type='correction'] {
    color: var(--evidence-mixed);
  }
  .revision-badge[data-type='update'] {
    color: var(--signal);
  }
  .revision-badge[data-type='published'] {
    color: var(--evidence-supported);
  }
</style>
